From 4d578b3af32d5e9ffc69117887216004219ca8a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 21:06:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] HostFS: the host's shared folders as a disk (virtio-fs)

A directory shared by the host (Apple Virtualization.framework's virtio-fs
device, 1AF4:105A, and Apple's FUSE server behind it) appears in Haiku as a
mounted volume, /HostFS by default, and behaves like a disk: mount_server
mounts it at boot, and it shows up on the Desktop, in Tracker's Mount menu
and in DriveSetup.

- drivers/disk/virtual/virtio_fs: the transport. Negotiates the device,
  moves FUSE requests through 8 DMA slots (hiprio queue for FORGET, one
  request queue), and publishes /dev/disk/virtual/virtio_fs/<n>/raw, a
  64 KiB read-only pseudo-disk whose first block names the device and its
  tag. A boot module: mass-storage PCI devices are only probed in the
  /dev/disk scan at boot.
- file_systems/hostfs: the FUSE client (protocol 7.31). Recognises the
  pseudo-disk, mounts it, and gets the transport through a kernel-only
  ioctl. FUSE node IDs are vnode IDs; a node table returns lookups with
  FORGET when the VFS drops a vnode and keeps directories while anything
  below them is known, so ".." resolves. Files go through the file cache
  (mmap and executables work) with close-to-open consistency: open
  revalidates size and mtime against what the cached pages were read with,
  close writes the pages back. Page I/O uses one host handle per node,
  opened read-write when the host allows it and released when the last
  file cookie goes away.
- mount_server mounts HostFS volumes whatever the automount settings say.
- The FUSE protocol header is Linux's include/uapi/linux/fuse.h, used under
  the BSD licence it carries for exactly that file.

Not yet: attributes (xattrs), host-side change notification, readdirplus.
